title The tree that assembles its own paths
prompt The baton, seat grass. Lap N 4023 by git rev-list. Prior lap's recommend: the .rye compile-reach census wants its witness and control next lap.
think Council rota row 3 by N mod 5 -- Water -- was the row the 20260906.040420 lap read, and the mark law says advance by hand past a repeat within a day. Read row 4 instead: Earth, the row that breathes in. Its threshold names the discipline plainly: the concrete fact outranks the plausible story, taken in at the door before any argument. Its fixed seat is Two Rooms, whose whole law is that a claim crosses into the checkable room by earning a witness. That is exactly the state of the census my last lap left -- a measured number sitting on the operator card with no instrument behind it -- so the rota chose the lap.
think Reds first. The cold roster opened at 122 green, 1 red, 3 gated, 718s. The red was index_row_bound: my own row 20260906.040420 sat below a peer's 040301 on the day shelf, an auto-merge seating it out of order -- the %440 class the card already names. Swapped the two rows through the original inode, witness GREEN, before touching anything constructive.
think Rebuilt the census as an instrument rather than a one-liner. The root grammar is a rye build/run/test verb whose token before it is the rye driver, with backslash continuations joined -- 1,270 roots. The elder inline reading used 1,468, and the gap is files a script merely NAMES: a path inside a comment, a path in a case exclusion pattern, a path in a for p in list. Naming a file does not compile it.
think That gave reached=1627 unreached=313 against the elder 1841/99, and neither reproduces the other. Rather than publish a third number I went to ground truth and asked, for the biggest unreached room, whether anything actually builds those files.
think tools/p/parity_ch01.rish holds let dir = "rye/tests", a list of 116 bare stems, and map witnesses as s: run [... rye "run" "${dir}/${s}.rye"]. The path of every program it runs exists only while the harness runs. rye/tests holds 116 files; the stem list holds 116; overlap 116; both differences zero. Every file my census called unbuilt is compiled on every parity pass, and so was every one the elder census called unbuilt, for the same reason.
think The second consequence is the one worth a guard. The suite asserts length failures == 0 over the list it holds rather than over the directory it names, so a test added to rye/tests and left off the list is never run while the suite still reports GREEN. Open, silent, and indistinguishable from success.
think The tree already keeps this rule twice -- crypto_tool_declaration_scan.sh reds a ghost_module, module_roster_scan.sh reds an unrostered file -- and neither reaches the largest assembled site it has. witness_reach_scan.sh had already noticed parity_ch01 stands on no roster; its targets stand outside too.
obs Fifteen import targets resolved to no tracked file and four looked like a genuine break in linengrow. All fifteen were explained and none was a tree red: three were MY engine resolving a symlink's imports at the symlink's directory rather than its target's, one is a symlink a witness creates at runtime into glow/.cache, eight are untracked staged app trees that exist on disk, and two are pen corpora built at runtime. The concrete check beat my own plausible story, which is the row's own discipline turned on the auditor.
obs I published 55.9% of build sites as assembled before checking, and it was wrong -- the pattern was matching prose like "rye build failed for RW-2". Bound to the driver token the honest reading is 210 of 2,113, 9.9%. The three counts are printed so they balance, 1,895 literal plus 210 assembled plus 8 prose, because a residue nobody can see is a residue nobody can question.
obs The instrument caught itself twice. A scan whose header and fault messages spell the shape it hunts read its own documentation as tree evidence -- three false shapes -- and then its own witness added a fourth that took the guard red against itself. The guard's three files are read past by name now, for the reason %458 already wrote down.
obs A performance red of my own, found by timing rather than trusting: the first shape ran one awk and one grep per script and measured 35s, which a tier lap guard spends twice a lap on eight ships. One awk over batched files plus a single grep -l naming the eleven candidates took it to 4s with every reading identical. The bisect found the cause honestly -- a sed edit I believed had landed had not, so the loop was still opening 3,073 files.
obs No orphan count ships. A reach census over a tree with 210 assembled build sites reports a floor and reads as a fact, so the card's 41 orphans is withdrawn rather than corrected. That is the Two Rooms door refusing to open in the direction it is usually pushed.
obs Folded %459 and %461 whole to a shelf to bring the ledger under its bound -- one family, both meters wrong about their own ground, kept as the elders of the one %462 continues.
obs THE SEND COLLIDED and I aborted rather than untangled. Six commits landed on xy while I worked, and the rebase hit five conflicts including two different folds of %459 -- a peer folded it to their shelf at 20260906.053902 while I folded it with %461 to mine at 051925. Resolving conflict markers over a ledger whose spine must read 1..N with no gaps is where a wrong answer looks right, so I aborted, saved my four new files, reset hard to xy/main, and rebuilt the ledger work against the tree that actually exists. %462 and %463 had both been published in the hour, so my row became %464 -- and on the NEXT fetch a peer had published %464 and %465 too, so it is %466. Four allocations, one stamp: 20260906.051753 named it in every tree the whole time. The second rebase I resolved rather than aborted, since its three conflicts were appends -- and it still cost a repair, because extracting my row from the conflict side pulled the row beneath it and seated %463 twice; the spine witness named it in one line.
obs AND THE RESET TAUGHT THE LAP ITS OWN LESSON AGAIN. On the new base a peer had landed rye_compile_reach at 20260906.045200 -- the very census this thread booked. I read it before booking, and my first row was too strong: I wrote that it missed the 114, and its card entry names them exactly, "114 tests in rye/tests/ no runner names". That sentence is literally true. What stands is narrower and I rewrote the row to say only that: the SET is called never and documented as a file no build reaches, and its header adds that what it names is genuinely uncompiled -- and for 114 of 179, 64 percent, a build does reach them every parity pass. The label and one header sentence are theirs to change; I priced them rather than editing their lane.
obs The mechanism is provable to the file and that is what makes the reading safe. Exactly two of the 116 escape the never set, mantra_weave_test and sha3_512_test, and each is the one a DIFFERENT runner spells literally, in mantra_weave_merge_witness.rish and parity-selftest.rish. 114 plus 2 is 116, so credit in that directory arrives only from a literal elsewhere and never from the harness that runs them all.
obs A peer had folded %461 to their own shelf while I folded it with %462 to mine, so I dropped my half -- theirs is published, mine was not -- and folded %462 alone. Folded that and two finished card accounts to another, to bring both pins under bound. The card closes at 40,910 of 40,960 -- 50 bytes -- so the next lap folds before it writes.
obs A find this lap made by adding to it: construction/archive stands at 259 flat entries, past the 256 every room is bounded at. It is on the advise roster so room_bound reports GREEN and books nothing, and my two shelf files took it 257 to 259. Every ship folds into that room every lap, so it grows fastest of any room in the tree and it is the one room whose growth is a side effect of the fold discipline itself. Named rather than taken: folding an archive room is its own round, and the fold shape for a room found by WHAT rather than WHEN is the letter fold.
obs %440 fired three times inside this one send and six times in the day. The reset carried one pair out of order; the later rebase left four stamps duplicated and two more pairs misordered. A dedupe-and-sort by hand repaired each, and that is the whole repair -- which is the argument for the writer the card already named and nobody has built. A lantern firing six times in a day, three of them in one send, is past what a hand should pay.
